WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.380
<v Maya>Welcome to the final part.

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:09.640
<v Maya>We have intensity as a percentage of maximum, the six factors, two ways to calculate a target heart rate, and the devices.

3
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.350
<v Maya>Now three things remain.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.850
<v Maya>How to estimate intensity without devices, the types of cardio training, and how to progress.

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.990
<v Maya>We end with a twelve question quiz.

6
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.240
<v Maya>Some clients should not be given a target heart rate number.

7
00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.810
<v Maya>Patients with heart, joint or neurological conditions.

8
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.130
<v Maya>Pregnant women.

9
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.480
<v Maya>People on special medication such as beta blockers.

10
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:35.840
<v Maya>The slides add cardiac and diabetic patients, and clients without access to monitors.

11
00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.010
<v Maya>Give a heart patient a target of one sixty, and they may end up somewhere else.

12
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.970
<v Maya>So these clients self regulate, because only they can say, this is still fine, beyond this is risky.

13
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.880
<v Dev>What's a beta blocker?

14
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:54.430
<v Maya>A medicine from cardiologists that artificially holds the heart rate down and never lets it pass a certain level.

15
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.270
<v Maya>If it caps at one hundred and the target is one forty, the heart rate will never get there.

16
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.930
<v Maya>So an experience based method is needed.

17
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.360
<v Dev>First method?

18
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:09.280
<v Maya>RPE, rating of perceived exertion, from the exercise physiologist Gunnar Borg.

19
00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.800
<v Maya>Rating, a score.

20
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.050
<v Maya>Perceived, the client's experience.

21
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.050
<v Maya>Exertion, the difficulty.

22
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.020
<v Maya>Think of a restaurant feedback form, zero to five.

23
00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.400
<v Maya>The Borg scale runs zero to ten.

24
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.780
<v Maya>Zero is rest.

25
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:23.280
<v Maya>One and two, easy.

26
00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.620
<v Maya>Three, moderate.

27
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.150
<v Maya>Four, sort of hard.

28
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.720
<v Maya>Five and six, hard.

29
00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.610
<v Maya>Seven and eight, really hard.

30
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.980
<v Maya>Nine and ten, maximal effort.

31
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.170
<v Maya>The slide names nine really really hard and ten maximal, so check your course material.

32
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.790
<v Dev>How is it used?

33
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.510
<v Maya>The trainer says, do this at three, moderate.

34
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.020
<v Maya>The client rates it.

35
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.050
<v Maya>Ideally the client says three.

36
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.010
<v Maya>If at a higher speed it feels more than three, that speed isn't right.

37
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.670
<v Dev>What if the client says five when the trainer expects three?

38
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.670
<v Maya>A mismatch, from two factors.

39
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.680
<v Maya>State of mind, like a birthday dinner that started badly and ended with a good rating.

40
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:05.220
<v Maya>In cardio, a client who just got a telling off message from the boss will find everything harder.

41
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:11.790
<v Maya>And environment, a broken air conditioner or poor ventilation makes a moderate activity feel like a five.

42
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.390
<v Dev>So it's subjective.

43
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.100
<v Dev>Isn't that a weakness?

44
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.270
<v Maya>It's the point.

45
00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:25.170
<v Maya>Like rating a movie, one person finds six kilometres per hour moderate, another finds it really easy, a person with a medical condition may say eight.

46
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.070
<v Maya>That's why it's an estimation.

47
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.210
<v Maya>The client has the authority to self regulate.

48
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.730
<v Dev>Second method?

49
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.330
<v Maya>The talk test, an observation method.

50
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.750
<v Maya>The trainer watches breathing and talking.

51
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.710
<v Maya>Low intensity, breathing normally, talking yes.

52
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.970
<v Maya>Moderate, breathing deep and often, still able to talk.

53
00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.440
<v Maya>High, breathing deep and quick, talking only briefly, half air, half word.

54
00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.950
<v Dev>Where does it help?

55
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.580
<v Maya>When the trainer is busy with another client.

56
00:02:51.630 --> 00:03:00.420
<v Maya>A heart patient is on treadmill three at low intensity, and the trainer asks the general trainer on duty to chat about yesterday's cricket match and watch.

57
00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.400
<v Maya>If replies are clear and breathing normal, fine.

58
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.650
<v Maya>If not, tell the personal trainer.

59
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:12.090
<v Maya>The E-book says it is meant for beginners and used with RPE and a monitor, so check your course material.

60
00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.330
<v Dev>Recap?

61
00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.660
<v Maya>Without devices, for patients, pregnant women and beta blocker users.

62
00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:20.860
<v Maya>RPE is experience based, zero to ten.

63
00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.020
<v Maya>The talk test is observation based, breathing and talking.

64
00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.520
<v Maya>How should cardio be done, non stop, or in phases?

65
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:30.840
<v Maya>Continuous, also called steady state, is non stop.

66
00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.680
<v Maya>Interval is in phases.

67
00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.880
<v Maya>Like driving, a highway is non stop, city traffic is in phases.

68
00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:44.140
<v Maya>Interval has subtypes, low or moderate intensity interval training, high intensity interval training, and Fartlek.

69
00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.160
<v Dev>Is there a common structure?

70
00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:52.020
<v Maya>Every graph has three phases, with intensity on the vertical axis and time on the horizontal.

71
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.790
<v Maya>The re warm up, rising.

72
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.600
<v Maya>The main or work phase, different for each method.

73
00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.290
<v Maya>The cool down, falling.

74
00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.320
<v Maya>Example, five minutes re warm up, twenty minutes main phase, five minutes cool down, thirty in total.

75
00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.550
<v Maya>Twenty is only an example.

76
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.940
<v Dev>Why re warm up, if we've done mobility drills?

77
00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.700
<v Maya>Mobility drills are the general warm up.

78
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.650
<v Maya>The re warm up is cardio specific, to reach the heart rate zone.

79
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:24.960
<v Maya>Sumit starts at ninety five, so he goes speed two, four, six, eight, and at eight his watch shows he's in his zone, after about five minutes.

80
00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.620
<v Dev>And the cool down?

81
00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:32.360
<v Maya>At the end of the main phase his heart rate is still in the zone, so he can't hit the emergency stop.

82
00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:37.020
<v Maya>It's to avoid syncope, dizziness or blackout when activity stops abruptly.

83
00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.030
<v Maya>There's a saying, calves are the second heart of the body.

84
00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.630
<v Maya>They pump blood back up from the lower body.

85
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:48.570
<v Maya>Stop abruptly, and for a fraction of a second that return halts, and the heart and brain miss that blood.

86
00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.110
<v Dev>Now the methods.

87
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.320
<v Maya>Continuous.

88
00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.290
<v Maya>Main phase is a flat line.

89
00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.740
<v Maya>The target range, one forty to one fifty two for Sumit, is held throughout.

90
00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.960
<v Maya>Speed and intensity stay fixed.

91
00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:04.460
<v Maya>Aerobic pathway, slow twitch fibres, RPE about three to four.

92
00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:05.900
<v Dev>And the second?

93
00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.940
<v Maya>LIIT and MIIT, low and moderate intensity interval training.

94
00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:12.700
<v Maya>The graph looks like buildings.

95
00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.390
<v Maya>The top is moderate, the gap between is low.

96
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.660
<v Maya>For a beginner, jogging is moderate and walking is low.

97
00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:23.030
<v Maya>Jog two to three minutes, then walk, jog, walk, jog, walk, jog.

98
00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.530
<v Maya>Four jog bouts and three walk bouts.

99
00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.160
<v Maya>For an intermediate, running is moderate and jogging is low.

100
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.510
<v Maya>Same pattern, different activities.

101
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.400
<v Maya>Speed varies, aerobic, slow twitch, RPE five to seven.

102
00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:40.670
<v Maya>The notes say the seven bouts add up to fourteen to twenty one minutes, so treat twenty as schematic.

103
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.950
<v Maya>HIIT, high intensity interval training, looks like mountains.

104
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.280
<v Maya>Peaks are sprints.

105
00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:47.920
<v Maya>Gaps are active rest.

106
00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.840
<v Maya>Four sprints, three active rests in the example.

107
00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:54.350
<v Maya>In the sprint, intensity goes above eighty five percent.

108
00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.620
<v Maya>It's anaerobic, because it can't last more than three minutes.

109
00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:00.390
<v Maya>A sprint is usually ten to thirty seconds.

110
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:05.600
<v Maya>It uses fast twitch fibres, brings an adrenaline rush, and RPE nine to ten.

111
00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.350
<v Dev>What's active rest?

112
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.950
<v Maya>Don't rest completely.

113
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.320
<v Maya>Do something at moderate intensity.

114
00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:16.820
<v Maya>You can't drop to walking, because the heart rate falls too low to get back above eighty five percent.

115
00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:22.720
<v Maya>You can't keep running, because intensity only drops to seventy to eighty percent, which limits your sprints.

116
00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:23.850
<v Maya>So jog.

117
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.310
<v Maya>The sprint to rest ratio is one to two or one to three.

118
00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.860
<v Maya>For a thirty second sprint, one minute of rest is one to two, one and a half minutes is one to three.

119
00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:36.070
<v Maya>In active rest the body goes aerobic, slow twitch.

120
00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.620
<v Maya>So aerobic, anaerobic, aerobic, anaerobic.

121
00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:41.700
<v Dev>Who can do HIIT?

122
00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:45.490
<v Maya>Not beginners, because they're limited to fifty to sixty percent.

123
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:51.080
<v Maya>And even intermediate or advanced clients, not more than twice a week, to protect against fatigue.

124
00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:58.940
<v Maya>The notes flag that sprints above eighty five percent belong to super advanced in the classification, which the lecture doesn't reconcile.

125
00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:04.700
<v Maya>Gym group classes called HIIT are often branding, and many are really MIIT.

126
00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:06.160
<v Dev>And Fartlek?

127
00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:07.820
<v Maya>Swedish for speed play.

128
00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:13.890
<v Maya>It's interval, but randomly up and down like a mountain range, with speed and direction varying as per need.

129
00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.120
<v Maya>Think of fielding in cricket, where you don't know the direction or speed of the ball.

130
00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:22.130
<v Maya>On the slide's track, green is jogging pace, blue running, red sprints.

131
00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:28.670
<v Maya>The athlete wears a chest strap, heart rate goes to the trainer's phone, and the trainer calls, jog, run, sprint.

132
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:31.860
<v Maya>The E-book lists it separately from interval training.

133
00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.320
<v Dev>And progression?

134
00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:34.490
<v Maya>Six ways.

135
00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.690
<v Maya>The first three are workload factors, speed, distance and duration.

136
00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.270
<v Maya>Then angle, flat to incline on a treadmill.

137
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.380
<v Maya>Resistance level, the knob on a bike or cross trainer.

138
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.960
<v Maya>And gradient, running uphill outdoors.

139
00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:49.200
<v Dev>How long should cardio be?

140
00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:50.740
<v Maya>It depends on the goal.

141
00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.530
<v Maya>Marathon training needs longer sessions.

142
00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:57.790
<v Maya>For strength or size, cardio is fitted in on non weight training days.

143
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.990
<v Maya>And a lower resting rate with cardio isn't only about stroke volume.

144
00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:09.430
<v Maya>Cardio lowers resting heart rate and raises stroke volume, and that improves overall cardiac output, mainly at high effort.

145
00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.500
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

146
00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:19.610
<v Maya>One, without devices, use RPE, zero to ten, or the talk test, for patients, pregnant women and beta blocker users.

147
00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.810
<v Maya>Two, every session has a re warm up, main phase and cool down, and the cool down prevents syncope.

148
00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.680
<v Maya>Three, continuous is flat, MIIT is buildings, HIIT is mountains.

149
00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:37.540
<v Maya>Four, HIIT is anaerobic sprints above eighty five percent, not for beginners, not more than twice a week.

150
00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:43.000
<v Maya>Five, progress by speed, distance, duration, angle, resistance and gradient.

151
00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.300
<v Dev>Now the quiz.

152
00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.160
<v Dev>Twelve questions, answers straight after.

153
00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:47.980
<v Maya>One.

154
00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.570
<v Maya>What does cardio mean?

155
00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.640
<v Maya>Heart, from the Greek cardium.

156
00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:52.510
<v Maya>Two.

157
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.580
<v Maya>Maximum heart rate formula?

158
00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.200
<v Maya>Two twenty minus age.

159
00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:57.090
<v Maya>Three.

160
00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.620
<v Maya>Which category is the elliptical trainer?

161
00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.010
<v Maya>Weight bearing, non impact based.

162
00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:02.890
<v Maya>Four.

163
00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.910
<v Maya>Which arteries carry blue blood?

164
00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.660
<v Maya>The pulmonary arteries.

165
00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:07.570
<v Maya>Five.

166
00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.010
<v Maya>VO₂ rest?

167
00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.260
<v Maya>Three point five millilitres per kilogram per minute, one MET.

168
00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.140
<v Dev>Six.

169
00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.170
<v Dev>Cardiac output formula and rest value?

170
00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.120
<v Dev>Stroke volume times heart rate, about five litres per minute.

171
00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:22.120
<v Dev>Seven.

172
00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.990
<v Dev>Speed for two kilometres in twelve minutes?

173
00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.810
<v Dev>Ten kilometres per hour.

174
00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:27.720
<v Dev>Eight.

175
00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.840
<v Dev>Moderate intensity as percent of maximum?

176
00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.670
<v Dev>Sixty to seventy.

177
00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.560
<v Maya>Nine.

178
00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:35.220
<v Maya>Karvonen formula?

179
00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:39.510
<v Maya>Two twenty minus age minus resting, times intensity, plus resting.

180
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:40.340
<v Maya>Ten.

181
00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:42.280
<v Maya>Sumit's Karvonen range?

182
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.270
<v Maya>One forty to one fifty two.

183
00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:45.220
<v Maya>Eleven.

184
00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.730
<v Maya>Which device is most accurate, and which is not recommended?

185
00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.800
<v Maya>Chest strap, and grip sensors.

186
00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:51.740
<v Maya>Twelve.

187
00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.120
<v Maya>Why cool down?

188
00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:54.960
<v Maya>To avoid syncope.

189
00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.760
<v Dev>That's the whole topic.

190
00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:58.570
<v Maya>Well done for sticking with it.

191
00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.610
<v Maya>Go and apply it with real clients.

